  summary: [
    'The Moon has its own gravity, so spacecraft orbit it in <b>ellipses</b> just like they orbit Earth — low lunar orbit is ~1.63 km/s, and lunar escape is ~2.38 km/s (much less than Earth’s, because the Moon is small).',
    'Which body you orbit is a <b>tug-of-war</b>. The Moon’s gravitational “territory” is its <b>Hill sphere</b>, radius ~61,500 km — roughly where the Moon’s pull stops beating Earth’s along the Earth–Moon line. Inside it you orbit the Moon; outside, the Earth.',
    'The <b>balance point sits ~85% of the way to the Moon</b>, not at the midpoint — this is the <b>L1 Lagrange point</b>, where the Moon’s gravity plus the orbital (rotating-frame) effect balance Earth’s pull. Release an object at rest closer than that and it falls back to Earth; beyond it, onto the Moon.',
    'The same handoff happens one level up: inside the edge of Earth’s <b>Hill sphere</b> (~1.5 million km) you orbit the Earth; beyond it, the Sun takes over.',
    '<b>xGEO</b> (“extended GEO”) is the region <b>beyond the GEO belt (~36,000 km) but inside Earth’s grip (out to the edge of Earth’s Hill sphere, ~1.5 million km)</b>. The Moon, at 384,400 km, sits right in it.',
    'The big skill is choosing a <b>reference frame</b>: <b>Earth-centred inertial (ECI)</b> (fixed to the stars — the Moon orbits Earth); <b>Earth–Moon rotating (synodic)</b> (the Earth–Moon line held fixed — ideal for studying transfers); <b>Moon-centred inertial (MCI)</b> (the same motion seen from the Moon); and <b>ECL-EMBR</b> — centred on the Earth–Moon <b>barycenter</b> and co-rotating in the ecliptic, the frame in which <b>both bodies freeze and the Lagrange points hold still</b> (the stage for Module 6).',
    'The Moon is just another <b>Earth satellite</b>: a slightly elliptical orbit (e≈0.055), inclined ~5° to the ecliptic, period one month.',
    'The Moon is <b>tidally locked</b> — the same face always points at Earth — so it rotates exactly once per orbit. (Why it’s locked is beyond our scope; see the resources.)',
    'Three planes are all tilted relative to each other: Earth’s <b>equatorial plane</b>, the <b>ecliptic</b> (Earth’s orbit around the Sun), and the <b>Moon’s orbital plane</b>. That’s why eclipses are occasional, not monthly.',
    '<b>Moon phases</b> come from the Sun–Earth–Moon geometry over the ~29.5-day cycle — the same illumination idea as a satellite lit by the Sun.',
    'Reaching the Moon means <b>leading it like a duck hunter</b>: you burn into a transfer orbit aimed at where the Moon <i>will be</i> when you arrive at apogee, not where it is now.',
  ],

